NAME
    bt - backtrace the live call chain

SYNOPSIS
    bt [<count>]
    bt reset
    bt <cpu> [<count>|reset]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the calls currently on the stack, innermost frame first: what was
    called, where it was called from, and which frame number the call
    happened on. This is the dynamic counterpart to 'callers' (see 'man
    callers'), and the two answer genuinely different questions. 'callers'
    scans code and reports every instruction that COULD reach an address;
    'bt' reports the one path that actually did, this time. When a routine
    has six static callers, 'callers' gives you six suspects and 'bt' gives
    you the answer.

    Frames are recorded from the CPU's own call and return opcodes plus its
    interrupt entry points, so an NMI or IRQ frame is labelled as such
    rather than looking like an ordinary call. Addresses are resolved
    through the label registry - see 'man label'.

ACCURACY
    A call stack tracked this way is an inference, not machine state, and
    it can drift on code that manipulates its own stack directly: a routine
    that pushes a return address and jumps, that pulls a return address it
    never intends to return through, or that unwinds several frames with a
    stack-pointer write rather than matched returns. Two symptoms give this
    away - a depth that only ever grows, and a rising 'unmatched returns'
    count, which 'bt' reports whenever it is nonzero.

    'bt reset' forgets the current chain and starts counting again from the
    current instruction. Do that after stepping through a routine known to
    play with its own stack, and before trusting 'step out' or a 'profile'
    run that has to survive it. Depth is also capped, so a runaway chain
    stops recording rather than growing without bound.

    Frames opened before the debug target attached are not on the stack -
    code already running at that moment shows up in 'profile' under
    '(outside any recorded call)' rather than under its real caller.

EXAMPLES
    bt
    bt 8
    bt reset

OTHER PROCESSORS
    'bt <cpu>' backtraces another chip - see 'man cpus'. Only a chip with a
    real call/return seam has a stack to report: on an SNES that is the
    main CPU and the SA-1, both 65816s. The GSU and the SPC700 report none,
    and say so rather than inventing frames.

SEE ALSO
